    "body": "I concur with matchai, for a several reasons:\r\n\r\n  - `sed` commands can be highly nonportable. The most obvious thing we have to watch out for is BSD sed, which is used by MacOS and other BSDs, but we have to watch out for other environments (e.g. busybox) that may be in use on platforms like iSH and Termux.\r\n  \r\n  (For one example of where this might be problematic, using `\\1` to refer to capturing groups appears to be a GNU sed extension which might not be generally available).\r\n\r\n  - If the command is botched (e.g. sometimes when copy-pasting, my terminal likes to drop a few escape codes in for reasons I have not yet identified), this could potentially lead to the entire starship.toml being lost, with no backups.\r\n \r\n  - This does not take into account starship config resolution rules, which may change in the future.",
